WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.408 --> 00:00:04.670
Mutation has become a
sort of genetic boogeyman.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.780
It's a common trope in horror.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.570
In people, mutations cause disfigurement,

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.360
aggression, even cannibalism.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.580
So it's not surprising that the
conversation around mutation

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.245
and the virus that causes
COVID-19 feels scary.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.830
But genetic mutations in real life

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.340
aren't like the ones we see in movies.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.670
And on a viral level, they could actually

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.910
help us track and manage COVID-19.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.060
So, let's break down what
mutations actually are

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.910
and what they could mean for the virus

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.501
we're facing right now.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:43.410
This is SARS-CoV-2, the
virus that causes COVID-19.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.090
It's a type of RNA virus,

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.570
some of which mutate practically
every time they replicate.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.530
SARS-CoV-2 is actually one
of the rare RNA viruses

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.270
that has a proofreading mechanism

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:58.400
that slows down its mutation rate some.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.358
Despite that, it has mutated,

00:01:00.358 --> 00:01:02.810
and it will continue to mutate.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.910
But that's not as scary as it sounds.

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:06.518
Mary Petrone: It's basically a typo,

00:01:06.518 --> 00:01:07.910
or a mistake that occurs,

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.030
like if you were writing an essay.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.620
Narrator: SARS-CoV-2's RNA is basically

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:16.330
a blueprint for making more SARS-CoV-2s,

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.070
but viruses can't replicate themselves.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.580
They have to hack into
another organism's cells

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.470
and use their machinery
to make new copies.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.490
Here's how that process works.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.937
Petrone: The virus will,
like, let its genetic material

00:01:28.937 --> 00:01:31.700
free into the cytoplasm of the cell,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.140
and then when that genetic
material encounters

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.960
what's called a ribosome...

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.230
Narrator: The ribosome
reads the virus' blueprint

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.600
and starts building a new virus.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.240
Petrone: Then it'll read it
three nucleic acids at a time,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.960
so we'd call that a
codon, and they're always

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.670
three nucleic acids.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.780
And a combination of three nucleic acids

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.490
corresponds to a single amino acid.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.560
Narrator: String those
amino acids together...

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.270
Petrone: And that's what
creates the protein.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.540
Narrator: But occasionally,
the wrong nucleic acid

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.570
will get added to the chain.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.250
This can sometimes, but not always,

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.330
lead to changes down the line,

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:06.920
making that offspring virus

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.150
slightly different from its parent.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.630
Each amino acid or
combination of amino acids

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.112
is responsible for defining

00:02:14.112 --> 00:02:16.800
some characteristic of the virus.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.560
Things like its shape,
how infectious it is,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.120
what kind of organism it infects,

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.980
and the types of cells it targets.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:27.390
So a mutation, or, more
likely, multiple mutations,

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.340
has the potential to
change any of those things.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.431
Petrone: Theoretically,
everything is fair game

00:02:32.431 --> 00:02:34.541
because these, you know, mutations

00:02:34.541 --> 00:02:37.210
are totally random events.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:39.110
Narrator: But a mutation in a single virus

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.550
only affects that one virus.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.100
For a mutation to stick, it has to be able

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.710
to be passed on to new
generations and new hosts.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.030
So something that messes
with vital functions,

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:53.800
like the virus' ability to
replicate, means a dead end.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.770
In terms of the virus' fitness,

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.090
the probability of beneficial, neutral,

00:02:58.090 --> 00:02:59.708
and harmful mutations

00:02:59.708 --> 00:03:02.600
shifts based on the environment it's in.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.310
Let's say a virus is perfectly
suited to its environment.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.310
The virus doesn't stop mutating,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.640
but it would be impossible for a mutation

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.140
to make it even more perfect,

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.570
so the likelihood of
mutations that are neutral

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.830
or harmful for the virus are very high.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.580
It's impossible to say SARS-CoV-2

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.330
is perfectly suited for infecting humans.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:25.930
But since it can move from person

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.540
to person so efficiently,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.603
experts don't think it's facing
a lot of pressure to adapt.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.660
Plus, its two most
concerning characteristics,

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.630
how contagious it is and
how harmful it can be,

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.090
are controlled by multiple genes.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.720
So in order to become more
contagious or more harmful,

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.810
the virus would need to undergo multiple

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:49.270
beneficial mutations at
exactly the right time.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:51.690
That's just not very likely.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.570
But Mary points out that
even talking about mutations

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:58.860
in terms of "dangerous" or
"worse" can be subjective.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.280
Petrone: In my mind,
what makes this outbreak

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.670
or this epidemic especially
concerning is that

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.560
so many people who get
infected with the virus

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:06.610
don't really show symptoms,

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.980
and that's why it's been
able to spread so far.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.667
But if there were to be
a change that, you know,

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:13.320
made the virus, the infection,
much worse in people,

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.020
in some ways, it'd be easier to control.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:16.720
So there are all of these
different trade-offs

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.310
we need to think about.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.380
Narrator: Mutations
can actually be pivotal

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:23.020
when it comes to fighting COVID-19.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.930
Since we know what
SARS-CoV-2's genome looked like

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.510
at the beginning of the outbreak,

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.650
we can track when and where it changes.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:33.300
Petrone: We actually saw that
patients that we identified

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.740
in Connecticut in the early
stages of the outbreak here,

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.820
their virus was more closely related to

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.100
viruses that were collected and sequenced

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.580
in Washington state compared to in China

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.270
or in Europe, for example.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:47.450
So, that actually tells us

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.870
that we have domestic
transmission going on,

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.530
and we are getting all this
information based off of

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.360
mutations that have arisen in the virus.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:56.590
Narrator: There are a couple initiatives

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:59.140
that are tracking this
data globally right now.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.340
One being Nextstrain,

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.660
an open-source project
that tracks the spread

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.030
and evolution of infectious
diseases in real time.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:10.170
Right now, it's focused on COVID-19.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.030
Researchers can use that information

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.530
to answer questions like:

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:15.263
How fast is it mutating?

00:05:15.263 --> 00:05:16.363
Not very.

00:05:16.363 --> 00:05:18.110
Is it spreading by air travel?

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:20.730
Definitely, especially early on.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:22.280
Will it mutate in a way that makes

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:25.970
a potential vaccine
ineffective, like the flu?

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:29.192
Petrone: People who design
the vaccines think about this.

00:05:29.192 --> 00:05:31.360
Like, this is something that's
factored into vaccine design.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.740
So, typically, vaccines, they use

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:35.660
these highly conserved targets.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:38.750
So, they'll pick some
part of the virus that

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:40.930
is not tolerant to a lot of change

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.030
or to really any change.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:45.250
It can't really mutate
away from the vaccine

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.140
without, you know, compromising some other

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:51.363
really crucial element of its life cycle.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:53.760
Narrator: Plus, the flu is unique

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.650
in the way its genetic
material is structured.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:00.770
It's an RNA virus too, but
its genome is segmented.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:03.360
The coronavirus genome is not.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.080
The segments translate to a
bunch of different proteins.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.800
So every time the body sees a new protein,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.860
it has to make an entirely different set

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:14.505
of antibodies specifically
designed to fight it.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.368
We don't know exactly
how often a new vaccine

00:06:18.368 --> 00:06:21.020
for COVID-19 will be necessary,

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.380
but we do know the virus
doesn't have the same

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:25.990
flexibility when it comes to proteins.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.540
And experts have been looking
at another coronavirus,

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:30.720
the one that causes
SARS, to get an idea of

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.820
how we might handle this one.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:36.630
Immunity to that virus lasts
roughly two to three years.

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.600
Since it and the new coronavirus

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.300
share a significant amount
of genetic material,

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.320
this could be a good estimate.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.760
If that's the case, then a
vaccine should last just as long

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.700
since the virus is mutating so slowly.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.610
And that's really the point.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:54.330
The viral ancestor of SARS-CoV-2

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.080
did have to mutate in order to
jump from animals to people,

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.470
but it most likely
happened very gradually,

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.904
with a series of mutations over the course

00:07:02.904 --> 00:07:05.660
of many, many years.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:08.800
Petrone: The timescale is
really what matters here.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.450
At the end of the day, we
need to be thinking about

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.320
how best we can control this outbreak

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.990
in the US and also around the world,

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.565
because if there's no
ongoing transmission,

00:07:17.565 --> 00:07:19.475
there's no mutations, and then we really

00:07:19.475 --> 00:07:20.650
won't have to worry.

